from pathlib import Path
import json,re,html
import pandas as pd
root=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
r=json.loads((root/'model_results/summary.json').read_text());m=r['metrics']
def figure(path,title,obs,interp,imp,note):
 return f'<article class="chart"><h2>{title}</h2><img src="{path}" alt="{title}" loading="lazy"><p class="note">{note}</p><p><b>Observation →</b> {obs}</p><p><b>Interpretation →</b> {interp}</p><p><b>Research Implication →</b> {imp}</p></article>'
eda=(root/'assignment5.html').read_text();eda_cards=[]
for i in range(1,11):
 block=re.search(fr'<section id="chart{i}">(.*?)</section>',eda,re.S).group(1)
 block=re.sub(r'src="data:image/png;base64,[^"]+"',f'src="eda_figures/chart_{i:02}.png" loading="lazy"',block)
 eda_cards.append('<article class="chart">'+block+'</article>')
table='<div class="scroll"><table><caption>All models evaluated on the same 2,324 test records</caption><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Test MAE ↓</th><th>Test RMSE ↓</th><th>Test R² ↑</th><th>Train MAE</th></tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join(f'<tr><th>{x["model"]}</th><td>${x["test_mae"]:,.0f}</td><td>${x["test_rmse"]:,.0f}</td><td>{x["test_r2"]:.3f}</td><td>${x["train_mae"]:,.0f}</td></tr>' for x in m)+'</tbody></table></div>'
comparison=table+'''<p class="note">MAE is average absolute dollar error. RMSE penalizes large errors more heavily. R² compares squared errors with variation around the test mean; it is not an accuracy percentage. Train MAE helps expose overfitting.</p><div class="model-grid"><article class="card"><h3>Linear Regression</h3><p>A weighted combination of vehicle features. Useful as a simple, interpretable starting model.</p><p>Unconstrained predictions produced 129 negative prices on the test set. Those predictions are retained in the reported scores.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Decision Tree</h3><p>Groups records using a sequence of feature splits. Captures nonlinear patterns.</p><p>Maximum depth 8; minimum 5 training records per leaf; random seed 42.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Random Forest</h3><p>Averages 120 trees to capture feature combinations and reduce reliance on one tree.</p><p>Maximum depth 18; minimum 2 training records per leaf; all features considered at each split; random seed 42.</p></article></div>'''
comparison+=figure('model_results/mae.png','Model comparison: average prediction error','Random Forest has test MAE $6,217, compared with $8,864 for Decision Tree, $10,849 for Linear Regression, and $19,728 for the median baseline.','All three trained models improve on the baseline in this split. Random Forest reduces MAE by approximately 68.5% relative to the baseline.','Repeat grouped evaluations before selecting a final model. One split does not establish stable superiority.','Same 8,875 training records and 2,324 test records for every model. Hyperparameters were fixed before this run; no tuning was performed.')
diagnostics=figure('model_results/predicted_actual.png','Predicted versus actual prices','Points away from the diagonal show prediction errors. Linear Regression produces 129 negative predictions.','The linear model is not constrained to positive prices. Prediction quality varies across the price range.','Test a log-price target and compare errors by price range using training-set validation.','Every test record is shown. Both axes use a symmetric-log scale with a linear region near zero (±$2,000), allowing negative predictions to remain visible. Dashed line indicates perfect prediction.')
diagnostics+=figure('model_results/residuals.png','Random Forest residuals','Random Forest RMSE is $13,485, substantially higher than its $6,217 MAE.','Some large errors remain despite a lower average error. Negative residuals mean underprediction.','Inspect expensive vehicles and source-price-floor records before making broad claims about performance.','Residual = predicted − actual MSRP. Symmetric-log axes show positive and negative values. All 2,324 test records are plotted.')
g=pd.read_csv(root/'model_results/group_errors.csv');rf=g[g.model=='Random Forest'];grp='; '.join(f'{row.vehicle_size}: ${row.mae:,.0f} (n={row.test_n})' for row in rf.itertuples())
diagnostics+=figure('model_results/group_errors.png','Model errors by vehicle size',f'Random Forest test MAE by size is {grp}.','An overall score hides differences across vehicle categories. Group sample sizes and price distributions also differ.','Evaluate group-specific reliability and add price-band comparisons in the final paper.','All test records, grouped by vehicle size. Group comparisons are descriptive and do not establish statistical significance.')
importance=figure('model_results/importance.png','Which features does Random Forest rely on?','Shuffling horsepower increases test MAE by about $14,474; shuffling year increases it by about $6,006.','In this fitted model and test set, horsepower and year carry substantial predictive information. Importance is not evidence of causation.','Check whether the year effect reflects source-price construction, and repeat the analysis after reviewing the $2,000 floor.','Permutation importance on the full held-out test set: mean MAE increase from three random shuffles per original input feature. Error bars show shuffle standard deviation, not confidence intervals. Correlated features can share or mask importance.')
methods=f'''<article class="chart"><h2>What was actually run</h2><ol><li><b>Data:</b> All {r['records']:,} cleaned records from Assignment 3. The target is original recorded MSRP in dollars. No global outlier removal, clipping, or target transformation was used.</li><li><b>Inputs:</b> year, horsepower, cylinders, make, vehicle size, vehicle style, transmission, and driven wheels. Model name is used only to define split groups. No MSRP-derived input columns are included.</li><li><b>Split:</b> GroupShuffleSplit with seed 42 reserves 20% of make–model groups for testing. This produced {r['train_records']:,} training records in {r['train_groups']} groups and {r['test_records']:,} test records in {r['test_groups']} groups. Group overlap is zero. Group proportions do not guarantee exactly 20% of rows.</li><li><b>Preprocessing:</b> Numeric medians, missingness indicators, and standardization are fitted only on training data. Categorical missing values use the training mode, followed by one-hot encoding with the first category dropped. Unknown test categories are encoded as all zeros. Genesis appears only in the test split, which limits interpretation for that brand.</li><li><b>Training:</b> Three regression models and a training-median baseline use the same split. Fixed model settings are documented above and in the Python script. No cross-validation or hyperparameter search was performed in this preliminary run.</li><li><b>Evaluation:</b> MAE, RMSE, R², prediction plots, size-group errors, and Random Forest permutation importance. The test scores were not used to tune these models.</li></ol><h3>Limits and next steps</h3><p>This single split is an exploratory benchmark. Random Forest train MAE is $3,063 versus test MAE $6,217, showing a generalization gap. Historical records and repeated specifications are not a representative sample of current transactions. The existing cleaning rules were defined before this split and used full-dataset quality checks. No price flags derived from those checks were used as predictors.</p><p>Next: run grouped cross-validation within the training data, evaluate log-price modeling, investigate price floors and outliers, inspect errors by price band and unseen brand, and reserve fresh evaluation data for any further development prompted by these test results. Then refine the dashboard and write the methods, results, visualization contribution, and conclusions for the final paper.</p><h3>Downloads and sources</h3><p><a href="model_results/metrics.csv" download>Metrics CSV</a> · <a href="model_results/test_predictions.csv" download>Test predictions</a> · <a href="model_results/split.csv" download>Split membership</a> · <a href="model_results/permutation_importance.csv" download>Feature importance</a> · <a href="run_models.py" download>Reproducible model script</a> · <a href="data_cleaned.csv" download>Cleaned data</a></p><p>Run <code>python run_models.py</code> with pandas, NumPy, Matplotlib, and scikit-learn installed. This run used scikit-learn {r['sklearn']}. The source-data SHA-256 is retained in <a href="model_results/summary.json">summary.json</a>.</p><p>Dataset: <a href="https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/CooperUnion/cardataset">Cooper Union, Car Features and MSRP, Kaggle</a>. Methods: scikit-learn documentation for <a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.model_selection.GroupShuffleSplit.html">GroupShuffleSplit</a> and <a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.inspection.permutation_importance.html">permutation importance</a>.</p></article>'''
